NNAAMMEE
     ssnnddiiooccttll — manipulate audio device controls

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ssnnddiiooccttll [--ddiimmnnqqvv] [--ff _d_e_v_i_c_e] [_c_o_m_m_a_n_d _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssnnddiiooccttll utility can display or manipulate controls of sndio(7) audio
     devices, like the output level.  The options are as follows:

     --dd      Dump the raw list of available controls and exit.  Useful as a
             debugging tool.

     --ff _d_e_v_i_c_e
             Use this sndio(7) audio device.

     --ii      Display characteristics of requested controls instead of their
             values.

     --mm      Monitor and display audio controls changes.

     --nn      Suppress printing of the variable name.

     --qq      Suppress all printing when setting a variable.

     --vv      Enable verbose mode, a.k.a. multi-channel mode.  By default con‐
             trols affecting different channels of the same stream are dis‐
             guised as a single mono control to hide details that are not es‐
             sential.

     If no commands are specified, all valid controls are displayed on stan‐
     dard output.  Unless --dd, --mm, --nn, or --ii are used, displayed lines are
     valid commands.  The set of available controls depends on the audio de‐
     vice.

     Commands use the following two formats to display and change controls re‐
     spectively:

           [group/]stream[channel].function
           [group/]stream[channel].function=value

     On the left-hand side are specified the control group (if any), the af‐
     fected stream name, and the optional channel number.  Examples of left-
     hand side terms:

           output.level
           output[0].level

     If the channel number (including the brackets) is omitted, the command is
     applied to all channels.

     Values are numbers between 0 and 1.  Two-state controls (switches) take
     either 0 or 1 as value, typically corresponding to the _o_f_f and _o_n states
     respectively.

     If a decimal is prefixed by the plus (minus) sign then the given value is
     added to (subtracted from) the current value of the control.  If "!" is
     used instead of a number, then the switch is toggled.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Increase the lleevveell control affecting all oouuttppuutt channels by 10% of the
     maximum:

           $ sndioctl output.level=+0.1

     Mute all oouuttppuutt channels:

           $ sndioctl output.mute=1

     Toggle the above mmuuttee control:

           $ sndioctl output.mute=!

     Allow audio recording and set all iinnppuutt channels to 50%:

           # sysctl kern.audio.record=1
           $ sndioctl input.mute=0 input.level=0.5

